  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nWe'll remain sandwiched between surface low pressure off to our \nsouth and surface high pressure off to our northeast on Wednesday. \nAn inverted trough will pivot to the west into eastern Ohio and \nfocus one last day of scattered showers there. Latest HREF \nprobability for measurable precip suggests that coverage peaks in \nthe early morning hours before shifting southwest and ultimately\ndying out as high pressure finally wins out with much drier air\npushing in from the north. The lower dew points have already \narrived in western Pennsylvania and will continue advecting \nwest, so expect that the day will be dry there. By late \nWednesday, we should see our whole area turn dry.\n\nThe column really dries out on Thursday and support for rain ceases \nas an upper low closes off across the Mid-Atlantic and places us on \nthe dry, northerly flow side. Ensembles surge much lower 850-700 mb \nRH values south, so clouds should scatter out and return some \nsunshine. Ensembles then point toward a dry weekend locally as a \ncoastal low develops off the Mid-Atlantic and shortwave ridging \nbetween that and a trough across the Dakotas becomes our dominant \nfeature. There remains a sliver of a chance that the coastal low \njogs farther west, and latest runs have suggested a trend in that \ndirection, which would bring some low PoPs as far west as Pittsburgh.\nThe latest ensemble QPF distribution has about 86% of the \nmembers keeping Pittsburgh dry with a tail of values as high as\n~0.4\" and thus the mean being drug way up above the 95th \npercentile. So, while there are a select few low probability high\noutliers, even in the worst case scenario for Pittsburgh, the \nrainfall totals are less than half an inch. Farther east at \nLatrobe to Indiana, a similar distribution exists with about \n0.6\" on the high end.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nSlow moderation of temperatures begins on Wednesday as the \ndrier air starts to scatter out clouds, but beneath the cold \nupper low and with persistent easterly flow, NBM advertises only a\n40-50% chance of >65F in the PA & WV lowlands with a 60-80% \nchance in southeastern OH. The ridges likely hold in the low \n60s. Those probabilities gradually tick up into the weekend when\nupper 60s and low 70s are favored to return. With that said, \nwe'll then flip from below normal highs to below normal lows as \nhigh pressure establishes, dew points crash, and skies clear \nout. Optimal radiational cooling conditions should peak Thursday\nnight when we even see some probability increase for lows <40F.\nThose peak at around 50% down the PA ridges and increase quite \na bit into eastern Preston and Tucker Counties up to 80-90%. \nWould not even be entirely surprised if the cold spots of Canaan\nValley come in near freezing.",
